{"created": "2023-06-08T18:29:49.187752+00:00", "modified": "2023-07-07T16:35:45.553320+00:00", "id": 8018664, "conceptrecid": "8018663", "doi": "10.5061/dryad.q573n5tph", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.5061/dryad.q573n5tph", "metadata": {"title": "Data for: Intracranial entrainment reveals statistical learning across levels of abstraction", "doi": "10.5061/dryad.q573n5tph", "publication_date": "2023-07-07", "description": "<div class=\"t-landing__text-wall \">\n<p>The following submission contains the data reported from the manuscript \"Intracranial entrainment reveals statistical learning across levels of abstraction\". The dataset was obtained from 8 neurosurgical patients who had intracranially implanted electrodes for seizure monitoring. Intracranial EEG (iEEG) data were recorded while the patients viewed a rapid stream of scene images. In the Category-Level Structured condition, patients viewed a series of trial-unique scene images, in which the categories of scenes were paired across repetitions (e.g., images of beach always followed by images of canyons). In the Exemplar-level Structured condition, participants viewed a sequence of 6 repeating scene images (from non-overlapping scene categories), which were paired across repetitions (e.g., image A always followed by image B). In the baseline Random condition, participants again viewed a sequence of 6 repeating scene images, but the images were presented in a random temporal order. Each image was presented for 250 ms, followed by a 250 ms inter-stimulus-interval period.</p>\n<p>The data presented here contain the raw iEEG data from the 8 patients during these task conditions, as well as information about the anatomical placement of their electrodes.</p>\n</div>", "access_right": "open", "creators": [{"name": "Sherman, Brynn", "affiliation": "University of Pennsylvania", "orcid": "0000-0002-5071-7101"}, {"name": "Aljishi, Ayman", "affiliation": "Yale University"}, {"name": "Graves, Kathryn", "affiliation": "Yale University"}, {"name": "Quraishi, Imran", "affiliation": "Yale University"}, {"name": "Sivaraju, Adithya", "affiliation": "Yale University"}, {"name": "Damisah, Eyiyemisi", "affiliation": "Yale University"}, {"name": "Turk-Browne, Nicholas", "affiliation": "Yale University"}], "keywords": ["intracranial EEG", "statistical learning"], "related_identifiers": [{"identifier": "10.1162/jocn_a_02012", "relation": "isCitedBy", "scheme": "doi"}], "resource_type": {"title": "Dataset", "type": "dataset"}, "license": {"id": "cc-zero"}, "communities": [{"id": "dryad"}], "relations": {"version": [{"index": 0, "is_last": true, "parent": {"pid_type": "recid", "pid_value": "8018663"}}]}, "notes": "<p>The iEEG files are in .edf (European Data Format). They can be loaded in by programs such as MATLAB (using the Fieldtrip or EEGlab toolboxes) or Python (using libraries such as MNE).</p>\n<p>The iEEG files also contain accompanying files (.json and .tsv) which can be read in by standard text editors.</p>\n<p>The electrode location files is a .csv which can be read in by standard text editors or spreadsheet programs, such as Excel.</p><p>Funding provided by: National Science Foundation<br>Crossref Funder Registry ID: http://dx.doi.org/10.13039/100000001<br>Award Number: </p><p>Funding provided by: Canadian Institute for Advanced Research<br>Crossref Funder Registry ID: http://dx.doi.org/10.13039/100007631<br>Award Number: </p>"}, "title": "Data for: Intracranial entrainment reveals statistical learning across levels of abstraction", "links": {"self": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/8018664", "self_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/8018664", "preview_html": "https://zenodo.org/records/8018664?preview=1", "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5061/dryad.q573n5tph", "self_doi": "https://doi.org/10.5061/dryad.q573n5tph", "self_doi_html": "https://zenodo.org/doi/10.5061/dryad.q573n5tph", "reserve_doi": "https://zenodo.org/api/records/8018664/draft/pids/doi", "parent": "https://zenodo.org/api/record